    "body": "The company that previously operated as Elbit Systems’ American subsidiary made its AUSA expo debut this week as Twenty-Six Defense.\nUnder the new brand, the Texas-based defense firm debuted Primex , a lightweight wearable meant to provide soldiers with night-vision capabilities and augmented reality so they can “maintain awareness without distraction or cognitive overload.”\nFor the design, Twenty-Six Defense said it engineered Primex for sustained operational use by equipping it with “the lightest head-borne load and near-neutral center of gravity.”\nThe company explained Primex uses low-light sensors, thermal imagery, and real-time processing to produce an ultra-low-latency digital perception that enables operators to better interpret the surrounding area even amid challenging environmental conditions.\nWhat separates Primex from other advanced lightweight heads-up display units for operators, according to Twenty-Six Defense, is that its design was built on mature technologies that have already been fielded.\nThe company said it already produces AN/PSQ-44 Enhanced Night Vision Goggles , which contain features that allow for rapid target acquisition and augment reality to enhance visual clarity.\nIn August, Ernie Basic, Twenty-Six Defense’s senior director of research and development, explained how his company was developing equipment for the Soldier Borne Mission Command , a project to deliver augmented reality wearables to soldiers.\nHe said the technology combines fighter-pilot displays with night-vision systems, so soldiers get “the full operational picture.”\n“You are not only seeing what is in front of you. You can leverage a feed coming from a drone or from other assets,” he said. “You can start seeing what is going on behind a house or another structure, cue a squad to a different location, and continuously update them.”\nIn addition to Primex, Twenty-Six Defense showcased defensive weapons and equipment like the XM1168 mobile 120mm mortar system, Autonomous Relocatable Surveillance Tower and SkyStriker Block 4E.\nAbout Daniel Terrill\nDaniel Terrill is a contributor to Military Times. He’s been reporting on military issues, the gun industry, and the outdoors for nearly two decades. Although writing is his passion, he’s been a Marine, police officer, and, perhaps the most dangerous job of his career, a substitute teacher.\nShare:\nMore In Land\nTrust between operators, CCAs starts with simulations: Former Air Force secretary\nSimulations and exercises will be key to boosting trust between operators and CCAs, according to former U.S. Air Force secretary Frank Kendall.\nCelebrating 40 years of Defense News\nSince 1986, Defense News has been the authoritative voice for the world's defense decision-makers.\nGuardsman learns to fly autonomous Black Hawk in less than an hour\nIn a training first, an Army National Guardsman used a tablet to control Sikorsky's optionally piloted Black Hawk and plan its task during an exercise.\nRecap: Highlights from the US Army’s 2025 conference in Washington\nThe Army continues to transform, with efforts focused on next-gen weaponry, long-range artillery, unmanned systems and much more.\nPentagon stages first ‘Top Drone’ school for operators to hone skills\nIndustry and military drone operators flew tethered and untethered first-person drones through a course designed to test endurance and maneuverability.",
